# Concurrency

Golden reference: `database.mdc` (`$transaction`), `async-reliability.mdc` (idempotency).

## Purpose

Two requests at the same time must not corrupt balances, inventory, credits, or reservations. **Read-then-write without isolation is a bug.**

## When this matters

Wallets, credits, billing, inventory, seat counts, reservations, status machines, “only one active subscription,” coupon redemption.

## Required patterns

| Pattern | Use when |
| ------- | -------- |
| `prisma.$transaction` | Multiple writes must succeed or fail together |
| `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` / serializable tx | Read balance → deduct balance in same transaction |
| `@@unique` constraint | One row per logical key; catch `P2002` → `ConflictError` |
| Conditional update | `updateMany({ where: { id, version } })` — check `count === 1` |
| Optimistic locking | `version` or `updatedAt` column on contested rows |
| Idempotent retry | Safe replays don't double-deduct (`async-reliability.mdc`) |
| Atomic increment | `update({ data: { credits: { decrement: amount } } })` with `where: { credits: { gte: amount } } }` |

```ts
// ❌ race — two requests both pass the check
const wallet = await prisma.wallet.findUnique({ where: { userId } });
if (wallet.balance < amount) throw new ValidationError("Insufficient balance");
await prisma.wallet.update({ where: { userId }, data: { balance: wallet.balance - amount } });

// ✅ conditional update — second request gets 0 rows updated
const result = await prisma.wallet.updateMany({
  where: { userId, balance: { gte: amount } },
  data: { balance: { decrement: amount } },
});
if (result.count !== 1) throw new ValidationError("Insufficient balance");

// ✅ transaction — inventory + ledger together
await prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
  const reserved = await tx.inventory.updateMany({
    where: { sku, quantity: { gte: 1 } },
    data: { quantity: { decrement: 1 } },
  });
  if (reserved.count !== 1) throw new ConflictError("Out of stock");
  await tx.orderLine.create({ data: { sku, ... } });
});
```

## Status transitions

Guard illegal transitions in the `where` clause — not only in application `if`:

```ts
const updated = await prisma.invoice.updateMany({
  where: { id, status: "pending" },
  data: { status: "paid", paidAt: new Date() },
});
if (updated.count !== 1) throw new ConflictError("Invoice already processed");
```

## Good examples

- Webhook payment: unique on `providerEventId` before side effects
- Coupon redeem: `@@unique([couponId, userId])`
- Credit spend: decrement with `gte` guard in one statement

## Bad examples

```ts
const item = await prisma.item.findUnique({ where: { id } });
if (item.stock > 0) await prisma.item.update({ data: { stock: item.stock - 1 } });
// two concurrent requests oversell
```

## Explicit prohibitions

- No read-modify-write on contested numeric fields outside a transaction or conditional update.
- No assuming single-threaded usage.
- No “check in service, update later” across separate requests without locking.
- No double side effects on concurrent webhook delivery without dedupe.

## AI guidance

Touching balances, inventory, credits, or one-time state? Name the race, pick transaction + unique constraint or conditional `updateMany`. Pair with idempotency for retries.
